[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Technical Tutorial: Distributed Consensus (Raft and Paxos) in Database Design"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In distributed database design, achieving fault-tolerant consensus is critical. When multiple nodes operate across a network, they must agree on a sequence of state machine updates despite network partitions, delays, and node failures. This tutorial examines the two foundational consensus algorithms: Paxos and Raft."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. The Consensus Problem"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The consensus problem involves multiple processes agreeing on a single data value or a sequence of values. Formally, a consensus algorithm must satisfy safety and liveness properties:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Agreement: Every non-faulty node must decide on the same value."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Validity: The agreed-upon value must have been proposed by one of the nodes."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Termination: Eventually, all non-faulty nodes decide on a value."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Paxos: The Theoretical Baseline"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Paxos, introduced by Leslie Lamport, operates on asynchronous networks. It uses roles: Proposers, Acceptors, and Learners. The Synod protocol (Basic Paxos) executes in two phases: Prepare/Promise and Accept/Accepted."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Phase 1a (Prepare): A proposer selects a unique proposal number n and sends a prepare request to a majority of acceptors."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Phase 1b (Promise): If an acceptor receives a prepare request with n greater than any seen before, it responds with a promise not to accept proposals lower than n, and returns the highest-numbered proposal it has already accepted, if any."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Phase 2a (Accept): If the proposer receives promises from a majority of acceptors, it sends an accept request for proposal (n, v), where v is the value of the highest-numbered proposal among the responses, or its own value if none were returned."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Phase 2b (Accepted): If an acceptor receives the accept request for (n, v), it accepts it unless it has already promised to consider a higher-numbered prepare request."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Raft: Understandability and Strong Leadership"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Raft was designed to be more understandable than Paxos by decomposing consensus into distinct sub-problems: Leader Election, Log Replication, and Safety. Raft relies heavily on a strong leader model."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Leader Election: Nodes are in one of three states: Follower, Candidate, or Leader. If followers don't hear from a leader, they timeout, increment the term, transition to candidate, and request votes."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Log Replication: The leader accepts client write requests, appends them to its log, and issues AppendEntries RPCs to all followers. Once replicated on a majority, the leader applies the entry to its state machine."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Implementation Blueprint (Raft AppendEntries Example)"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Below is a conceptual Python representation of how a Raft follower handles an AppendEntries RPC, verifying terms and log consistency before appending."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"def handle_append_entries(term, leader_id, prev_log_index, prev_log_term, entries, leader_commit):
    if term < current_term:
        return {"term": current_term, "success": False}

    if len(log) <= prev_log_index or log[prev_log_index].term != prev_log_term:
        return {"term": current_term, "success": False}

    # Append any new entries not already in the log
    for entry in entries:
        if entry not in log:
            log.append(entry)

    if leader_commit > commit_index:
        commit_index = min(leader_commit, len(log) - 1)
        apply_to_state_machine(commit_index)

    return {"term": current_term, "success": True}"},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"5. Paxos vs. Raft Comparison in Database Architecture"},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Decomposition: Paxos can be viewed as symmetric where values are proposed concurrently, whereas Raft enforces a strict leader-based architectural workflow."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Multi-Paxos vs. Multi-Raft: Multi-Paxos requires optimization layers for steady-state log replication, while Raft builds multi-paxos mechanics directly into its core design."},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modern distributed databases (like CockroachDB or TiKV) often use Multi-Raft or variants of Paxos (like Google Spanner's Paxos implementation) to shard data safely across clusters, guaranteeing linearizability and fault tolerance."}]
